  answer: 4,
  explanation: "**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Tying shoelaces requires advanced fine motor coordination and typically develops around 5 years old.\n\n---\n\nThis is the same 1-to-5-year fine motor ladder used in `pedep2-nd-8`, where the progression through the earlier rungs (and a divergence between the printed box and the course table over copying a square) is set out in full. Tying shoelaces unaided needs bilateral hand coordination and a multi-step sequence well beyond copying a shape, so it sits at the oldest, 5-year rung of the ladder — not itemized as a specific age in the cached milestone table itself, so this exact skill is general pediatric knowledge, not taken from the course material, though it is consistent with the table's overall picture of increasingly complex fine-motor sequencing by school age.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
