  explanation: "**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Hall’s criteria are used clinically to help identify Down syndrome, and upward slanted palpebral fissures are one of the classic facial features included.\n\n---\n\nHall's criteria are a ten-item bedside checklist (hypotonia, poor Moro reflex, joint hyperextensibility, excess nuchal skin, flat facial profile, upward slanting palpebral fissures, anomalous auricles, pelvic dysplasia, dysplasia of the mid phalanx of the fifth finger, and a single palmar crease) used to flag a newborn for karyotype confirmation before the result is back *(not taken from the course material; the deck describes these individual facial and physical features of Down syndrome — `6) Genetically determined disease_.txt` — without naming Hall's criteria as a set)*. Upward-slanting palpebral fissures sit on that list, which is what the printed answer is keyed to.\n\nThe other options are real, well-documented Down syndrome features that the deck itself lists — epicanthic folds, Brushfield spots (speckled irises) and mild microcephaly all appear in its craniofacial description — but none of them is one of Hall's ten items, and “three fontanels” is not a recognised Down syndrome sign at all; the neonate's fontanelles are typically wide and late to close in Down syndrome, not tripled in number. The distinction being tested is narrow: which listed feature belongs to this specific named checklist, not which features occur in Down syndrome generally.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
